a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"82fa3975-e119-4a8e-9676-a60b0cf8478c","timestamp":"2026-10-02T21:58:07.170Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)\n## Today\nFriday, October 02, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n**Context:** Mac evening session — checked whether the 5 Claude Code mods synced from Windows were active on the Mac, then enabled them.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked if \"the Claude mods we made earlier\" were visible. Answer: the files were present at `~/.claude/mods/` (`repo-band`, `next-steps`, `job-watch`, `merge-cleanup`, `where`), synced over via the `~/.claude` repo, but not loaded — `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS` was unset on the Mac.\n- Clarified a boundary: mods render UI (bands, buttons, status line, toasts) to the user's screen only. Their output does not enter the agent's context, so the user sees them and Claude does not.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- User approved enabling the mods on the Mac. Added the `export CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS=...` line (all 5 mod paths, colon-separated) to `~/.zshrc`, with a backup of the prior file at `~/.claude/jobs/a70d9b79/tmp/zshrc.bak`.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS` is per-machine by design (per the mods README) — it was only ever set on Windows, so repo-synced mods arrive dormant on a new machine. Expect this on any future machine.\n- `job-watch` is cross-platform: it reads Mac jobs via `launchctl`, so no Mac-specific fix was needed despite Mac/Windows storing scheduled jobs differently.\n- All 5 mods passed `claude plugin validate` on the Mac.\n- An already-open terminal will not pick up the new env var — a fresh terminal window is required.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- User must quit Claude Code, open a **new** terminal window, and start `claude` to load the mods. Expected: `repo-band` dirty/unpushed band, `next-steps` buttons, `job-watch` status line, and the `/repos`, `/jobs`, `/where` commands.\n- If nothing appears, check the Claude Code \"rollout switch\" that can block mods (README notes it was only observed blocking headless runs, not normal sessions).\n- Startup hook failures still open: `perch-pull` exit 1 (log at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json`), `daily-backup` errors `repo-behind-blocked:global-memory` and `manifest-unmanaged:42Analytics`.\n- `~/.zshrc` change is machine-local and not covered by the `~/.claude` repo sync — the Windows laptop needs no action, but a future machine will.\n\n### Memory Flush (16:53)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Memory Flush (16:53)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n## Today\nFriday, October 02, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n**Context:** Mac evening session — checked whether the 5 Claude Code mods synced from Windows were active on the Mac, then enabled them.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked if \"the Claude mods we made earlier\" were visible. Answer: the files were present at `~/.claude/mods/` (`repo-band`, `next-steps`, `job-watch`, `merge-cleanup`, `where`), synced over via the `~/.claude` repo, but not loaded — `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS` was unset on the Mac.\n- Clarified a boundary: mods render UI (bands, buttons, status line, toasts) to the user's screen only. Their output does not enter the agent's context, so the user sees them and Claude does not.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- User approved enabling the mods on the Mac. Added the `export CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS=...` line (all 5 mod paths, colon-separated) to `~/.zshrc`, with a backup of the prior file at `~/.claude/jobs/a70d9b79/tmp/zshrc.bak`.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS` is per-machine by design (per the mods README) — it was only ever set on Windows, so repo-synced mods arrive dormant on a new machine. Expect this on any future machine.\n- `job-watch` is cross-platform: it reads Mac jobs via `launchctl`, so no Mac-specific fix was needed despite Mac/Windows storing scheduled jobs differently.\n- All 5 mods passed `claude plugin validate` on the Mac.\n- An already-open terminal will not pick up the new env var — a fresh terminal window is required.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- User must quit Claude Code, open a **new** terminal window, and start `claude` to load the mods. Expected: `repo-band` dirty/unpushed band, `next-steps` buttons, `job-watch` status line, and the `/repos`, `/jobs`, `/where` commands.\n- If nothing appears, check the Claude Code \"rollout switch\" that can block mods (README notes it was only observed blocking headless runs, not normal sessions).\n- Startup hook failures still open: `perch-pull` exit 1 (log at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json`), `daily-backup` errors `repo-behind-blocked:global-memory` and `manifest-unmanaged:42Analytics`.\n- `~/.zshrc` change is machine-local and not covered by the `~/.claude` repo sync — the Windows laptop needs no action, but a future machine will.\n\n### Memory Flush (16:53)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Memory Flush (16:53)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d9a4f264-3b70-42fb-9429-0768994631a1","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"82fa3975-e119-4a8e-9676-a60b0cf8478c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c09993a7-f2cf-4adb-a14a-f014e5a24063","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Gotchas accumulate over time.** After every skill failure, add the lesson.\n\n### BPE (Bitter-Pilled Engineering) Check\n\nBefore finalizing any skill, ask: **\"Would a smarter model make this skill unnecessary?\"**\n\n- **Anti-fragile (keep):** Verification harnesses, data pipelines, tool wrappers, accumulated gotchas, deterministic scripts\n- **Fragile (question):** CoT orchestrators, format parsers, retry cascades, elaborate reasoning scaffolding\n\nFocus skills on knowledge Claude can't derive (failure modes, API quirks), tools Claude can't replicate (API calls, automation), and workflows that benefit from consistency.\n\n### Progressive Disclosure (from Anthropic)\n\nThree levels of information loading — use this to manage large skills:\n1. **Level 1 (YAML frontmatter):** Always in system prompt. Triggering info only.\n2. **Level 2 (SKILL.md body):** Loaded when skill is invoked. Routing + key guidance.\n3. **Level 3 (Reference files):** Root-level `.md` files or `References/` subdirectory loaded on demand.\n\nTell Claude what files exist; it will read them when appropriate. SKILL.md should be under 500 lines — if over, extract detailed content to reference files.\n\n### Testing Best Practices (from Anthropic)\n\nThree testing levels for skills:\n1. **Manual testing** — Run queries and observe behavior\n2. **Scripted testing** — Automate test cases (use TestSkill workflow)\n3. **Programmatic testing** — Build evaluation suites (use Evals skill)\n\n**Evaluation-driven development:** Define what \"this skill working\" looks like before building the skill. Iterate on a single challenging task until Claude succeeds, then extract the winning approach.\n\n### On-Demand Hook Pattern (from Anthropic)\n\nSkills can include hooks that activate only when invoked, remaining effective for the session:\n- `/careful` — Intercept dangerous commands (rm -rf, DROP TABLE, force-push)\n- `/freeze` — Block edits outside specific directories\n- `/audit` — Log all tool calls for session review\n\n*All guidance above derived from Thariq Shihipar's \"Lessons from Building Claude Code\" (Mar 2026), Anthropic's official skill guide, and platform documentation.*\n\n## Examples\n\n**Example 1: Create a new skill from scratch**\n```\nUser: \"Create a skill for managing my recipes\"\n→ Invokes CreateSkill workflow\n→ Reads SkillSystem.md for structure requirements\n→ Creates skill directory with TitleCase naming\n→ Creates SKILL.md, Workflows/, Tools/\n→ Suggests running TestSkill to verify effectiveness\n```\n\n**Example 2: Fix an existing skill that's not routing properly**\n```\nUser: \"The research skill isn't triggering - validate it\"\n→ Invokes ValidateSkill workflow\n→ Checks SKILL.md against canonical format\n→ Verifies TitleCase naming and USE WHEN triggers\n→ Reports compliance issues with fixes\n```\n\n**Example 3: Test if a skill actually helps**\n```\nUser: \"Test the Blogging skill to see if it's effective\"\n→ Invokes TestSkill workflow\n→ Generates 3 realistic test prompts\n→ Spawns with-skill and baseline agents in parallel\n→ Compares outputs, presents results\n→ Iterates with ImproveSkill based on feedback\n```\n\n**Example 4: Skill isn't triggering on relevant prompts**\n```\nUser: \"The Security skill doesn't trigger when I ask about pentesting\"\n→ Invokes OptimizeDescription workflow\n→ Generates 20 should/shouldn't-trigger queries\n→ Tests description accuracy via subagents\n→ Rewrites description, re-tests, reports improvement\n```\n\n**Example 5: Improve a skill that produces weak output**\n```\nUser: \"The research skill output is too verbose — improve it\"\n→ Invokes ImproveSkill workflow\n→ Reads skill + user feedback\n→ Diagnoses root cause (over-specified instructions)\n→ Rewrites with reasoning instead of rigid MUSTs\n→ Suggests TestSkill to verify improvement\n```\n\n## Gotchas\n\n- **Descriptions cap at 1024 chars, and this file's own description sat at ~1022 — 2 to spare** (2026-06-13 audit). It was cut to ~770 on 2026-08-15 by moving enforcement prose to the `## Enforcement` section; every USE WHEN trigger was kept, because descriptions are what make a skill fire. Re-measure before saving any frontmatter edit, and never buy headroom by deleting triggers.\n- **Two `## Workflow Routing` headers exist in this file** — the first (~l.284) is inside the embedded template example, the second (~l.323) is the real one. Header-scanning tools must take the LAST match, not the first. Match on line-anchored `^## Workflow Routing`; a plain substring search also hits an inline mention in the UpdateSkill instructions and returns three.\n- **Reading this skill's workflows and executing the steps by hand is the handrolling anti-pattern** — `skills/CLAUDE.md` requires invoking the skill, not imitating it. The skill that mandates Gotchas sections went without one until 2026-06-13; treat checklist items as applying to this skill too.\n\n## Execution Log\n\nAfter completing any workflow, append a single JSONL entry:\n\n```bash\necho '{\"ts\":\"'$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)'\",\"skill\":\"CreateSkill\",\"workflow\":\"WORKFLOW_USED\",\"input\":\"8_WORD_SUMMARY\",\"status\":\"ok|error\",\"duration_s\":SECONDS}' >> ~/.claude/LIFEOS/MEMORY/SKILLS/execution.jsonl\n```\n\nReplace `WORKFLOW_USED` with the workflow executed, `8_WORD_SUMMARY` with a brief input description, and `SECONDS` with approximate wall-clock time. Log `status: \"error\"` if the workflow failed.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Create a project-level skill at ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md per the approved plan (Task 6 Step 4): frontmatter is fixed by the plan; body is an 8-part read-only audit procedure. Load authoring rules only; the file is written after an in-progress code inspection of KeystoneOS finishes.\n\n**Assistant:** The checker is now clean on every test:\n- 16 of 16 fixtures behave as expected.\n- It agrees with the real YAML parser on all 42 real skills.\n- The live roster fails only the 7 switched-off skills.\n- All 4 fail-closed cases exit 2, and all older fixtures still behave as before.\n\nThese three are independent, so all go out now:\n- Commit and push the checker fix (KeystoneOS).\n- Write the Gate B round 2 dispositions.\n- Add Gate B round 2 to the execution log.\n\n**User:** do it\n\n**Assistant:** You said \"do it\", so I am running the three steps now:\n- Commit and push the checker.\n- Write the Gate B round 2 dispositions.\n- Log round 2 in the execution record.\n\n**Assistant:** API Error: Your computer went to sleep mid-response. The response above may be incomplete.\n\n**Assistant:** API Error: Can't reach the API server — check your internet or DNS (ENOTFOUND)\n\n**User:** resume\n\n**Assistant:** I paused, but I could not run the handoff skill. That skill is set so only you can start it; I am not allowed to. Please type `/mattpocock-skills:handoff` yourself.\n\n**Where the work stands:**\n- **Tasks 1–7 are done and pushed.** The last commits are `7459560` (claude-skills) and `08bf6e8` (KeystoneOS).\n- **Gate B:** every finding is fixed except the roster decision, which is yours. The last manifest fix `7459560` passed my tests, but Codex has not re-inspected it.\n- **Gate C:** Codex returned REVISE with 3 medium findings.\n  - C-01 (scripts not executable in git) is fixed and staged in KeystoneOS, but not committed.\n  - C-02 (lock race) and C-03 (usage counts cover Claude only) are not started.\n- **Codex quota** is used up until 10:41 PM, so no more Codex reviews can run before then.\n- **Task 8 (Pi trial)** is blocked: step 1 needs `sudo`, which needs your password. A BLOCKED report is drafted at `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/t8-pi-trial.md`, but not committed.\n- **Gate D** has not run.\n\n**Decisions for you:**\n\n1. **Roster (7 skills off in Claude but live in Codex and Pi):** ArXiv, ISA, Science, capture-insight, library-review, pp-espn, session-miner.\n   - Option A: retire them from canonical. None was used in Claude Code, which makes sense, since they are off there.\n   - Option B: turn them back on in Claude. Codex read each one's SKILL.md in 1–4 sessions, but some of that may be bulk scans, not real use.\n   - I recommend **A**, unless you use `library-review` or `pp-espn` in Codex.\n2. **Task 8:**\n   - Option A: run step 1 yourself. The command block is in the draft report above.\n   - Option B: leave it as BLOCKED.\n   - I recommend **A**, when you have 2 minutes.\n\nneeds input: run `/mattpocock-skills:handoff` yourself (I cannot start that skill), then tell me to resume and pick an option for the roster and for Task 8.\n\n**User:** <command-message>mattpocock-skills:handoff</command-message>\n<command-name>/mattpocock-skills:handoff</command-name>\n\n**User:** Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/mattpocock-skills/1.2.3/skills/productivity/handoff\n\nWrite a handoff document summarising the current conversation so a fresh agent can continue the work. Save to the temporary directory of the user's OS - not the current workspace.\n\nInclude a \"suggested skills\" section in the document, which suggests skills that the agent should invoke.\n\nDo not duplicate content already captured in other artifacts (specs, plans, ADRs, issues, commits, diffs). Reference them by path or URL instead.\n\nRedact any sensitive information, such as API keys, passwords, or personally identifiable information.\n\nIf the user passed arguments, treat them as a description of what the next session will focus on and tailor the doc accordingly.\n\n**Assistant:** The handoff skill is running now. It writes one file to your system temp folder, not to the workspace.\n\n**Assistant:** I wrote the handoff and checked that the file saved. It links to the plan, the execution log and the reports instead of copying them. It has no secrets or personal data.\n\nIt covers:\n- **Where the work stopped:** Tasks 1–7 are pushed. The executable-bit fix (C-01) is staged but not committed.\n- **The next 6 steps, in order:**\n  1. Finish the Gate C fixes (C-01 commit, C-02 lock race, C-03 Claude-only usage counts).\n  2. Codex re-inspection after the quota resets at 10:41 PM.\n  3. Log Tasks 6–7, including the honest smoke-test result.\n  4. Task 8: commit the BLOCKED report, or run it if `piuser` exists.\n  5. Gate D.\n  6. Update `STATUS.md`.\n- **Your two open decisions:** the 7 switched-off skills, and whether to run Task 8 step 1 with `sudo`.\n- **Traps from this session,** plus suggested skills for the next agent.\n\nTo continue in a new session, point it at the file:\n`/var/folders/gw/8qg6y2bj7rvdqzttmxfl9lx00000gn/T/handoff-2026-09-13-aios-gate-c.md`\n\nresult: Handoff written to /var/folders/gw/8qg6y2bj7rvdqzttmxfl9lx00000gn/T/handoff-2026-09-13-aios-gate-c.md — work paused mid Gate C, with 2 decisions waiting on you"},"uuid":"be7d1443-7705-46b9-b432-4b0526147058","timestamp":"2026-10-02T21:58:08.700Z","permissionMode":"auto","prompt